    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Bronchiectasis (J47.9)</content>. Stable on airway clearance. Mycobacterial cultures are final and negative at eight weeks, for both tuberculosis and nontuberculous mycobacteria. The sputum culture sent last week grew normal flora only. [S]<list><item>Continue airway clearance twice daily.</item><item>Pneumococcal vaccine at the preventive visit next month.</item><item>Spirometry and mycobacterial culture at one year.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Long-term opioid therapy: second step (Z79.891)</content>. The first step was well tolerated. She chooses a second small step. [S]<list><item>Morphine solution 4 mL (8 mg) twice daily from today.</item><item>Hold this dose at least four weeks, longer if she wishes.</item><item>Prescription monitoring program checked.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Prolonged grief disorder (F43.81)</content>. PHQ-9 7 and GAD-7 5, improving after twelve sessions; no suicidal ideation. [S]<list><item>Finish the sixteen sessions.</item><item>Safety plan reviewed.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Vitamin D deficiency, resolved (E55.9)</content>. 25-hydroxyvitamin D 34 ng/mL after twelve weeks. [S]<list><item>Vitamin D3 1000 to 2000 units daily long term; recheck yearly.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Mycobacterial culture including nontuberculous mycobacteria</td><td>At diagnosis (final at six to eight weeks), then yearly and with unexplained decline</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Final at eight weeks: no growth of Mycobacterium tuberculosis or nontuberculous mycobacteria; next yearly</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Sputum bacterial culture</td><td>At each review and at every exacerbation, before antibiotics</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Normal flora only</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Airway clearance technique and exercise</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Twice daily; sputum about a tablespoon a day</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Exacerbations: number in the past year, written plan, and rescue antibiotic</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>None since diagnosis</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Hemoptysis: amount, and when to seek emergency care</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>None since the first month</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>Spirometry and Bronchiectasis Severity Index</td><td>At diagnosis, then yearly</td><td>Up to date</td><td>Next at one year</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Opioid benefits and harms: function, sleep, cough, sedation, breathing, constipation, and her goals</td><td>Every visit, and at least every three months</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No sedation; sleep and function better; second step chosen</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Dose reduction at her request: withdrawal symptoms and sleep</td><td>Four weeks after each step</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Three restless nights after the first step, then settled; no extra doses</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Prescription drug monitoring program check</td><td>With every prescription, and at least every three months</td><td>Completed today</td><td>One prescriber, one pharmacy</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Urine drug test</td><td>At the start of review, then at least yearly</td><td>Up to date</td><td>Consistent with morphine only at intake; next at one year</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Naloxone at home, and no benzodiazepines or other sedatives</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Naloxone in date at home; no sedatives</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>PHQ-9</td><td>At least monthly during active treatment (CMID)</td><td>Completed today</td><td>7, from 10; GAD-7 5</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Suicide risk review and safety plan</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No suicidal ideation; plan current</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Grief therapy attendance and response</td><td>Weekly for sixteen sessions</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Twelve sessions attended</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>25-hydroxyvitamin D</td><td>Twelve weeks after starting replacement, then yearly; target 30 ng/mL or more (CMID)</td><td>Completed today</td><td>34 ng/mL; next yearly</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Weight and BMI</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>43.3 kg, BMI 17.6</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>Tuberculosis excluded and a diagnosis for the cough within two weeks. Outcome by 2026-03-23: Smears, NAAT and IGRA negative within a week; bronchiectasis diagnosed by CT at one week [S]</item><item>Airway clearance twice daily, with less sputum and no blood in it, by three months. Outcome by 2026-06-15: Airway clearance twice daily; sputum down from about four to one tablespoon a day; no blood since the first month [S]</item><item>Safe opioid therapy: naloxone at home, no overdose or sedation, and any dose change only by her choice. In progress; target 2026-10-19 [S]</item><item>Response of grief and depressive symptoms (PHQ-9 down by at least half) by six months. In progress; target 2026-10-19 [S]</item><item>Weight up toward a BMI of 18.5 (45.6 kg) by seven months. In progress; target 2026-10-19 [S]</item><item>Vitamin D above 30 ng/mL by twelve weeks. Outcome by 2026-06-15: 34 ng/mL [S]</item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: British Thoracic Society Guideline for Bronchiectasis in Adults (2019); Official American Thoracic Society/Infectious Diseases Society of America/Centers for Disease Control and Prevention Clinical Practice Guidelines: Diagnosis of Tuberculosis in Adults and Children (2017); Treatment of Nontuberculous Mycobacterial Pulmonary Disease: An Official ATS/ERS/ESCMID/IDSA Clinical Practice Guideline (2020); CDC Clinical Practice Guideline for Prescribing Opioids for Pain, United States, 2022 (2022); Shear MK, et al. Optimizing Treatment of Complicated Grief: A Randomized Clinical Trial (2016); VA/DoD Clinical Practice Guideline for Assessment and Management of Patients at Risk for Suicide (2024); CMID: Clinical Monitoring Intelligence Database, depression and osteoporosis monographs (2026)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Preventive visit next month. Seven-month review in the autumn with the PHQ-9, sputum culture, the influenza vaccine and the next opioid decision. Emergency care for more than a few teaspoons of blood. Next scheduled visit 2026-07-13: periodic adult preventive visit. [S]</paragraph></text>

